<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.7.4: Is the Polynomial Completely Factored?</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01693</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.7.4: Is the Polynomial Completely Factored?</md:title>
    <md:uuid>708efd8f-389e-438f-8142-87fe362d9e09</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Explain what it means to factor a polynomial completely.
    
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> To factor a polynomial completely means that every factor you have found, or the polynomial itself, is prime.
  </item>
</list>
<para>For questions 2 - 7, determine if each polynomial is completely factored. If the answer is no, write the completely factored form.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(3x(8x^2 − 9x − 3)\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>yes
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    60x^2y − 75xy + 30y\)
    
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>no;
    \(15y(4x^2 − 5x + 2)\)
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(2c(4c^2 + 20cd + 81d^2)\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>yes
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(5n^2 + 5n + 12\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> yes
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(5q^2 − 15q − 90\)
    
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> no;
    \(5(q + 3)(q − 6)\)
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    \(64x^2 + 16xy + y^2\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>no;
    \((8x + y)^2\)
  </item>
</list></section>
</content>
</document>
